###  http://purl.org/cs2013/LO/Support_for_concurrency_is_a_f
<http://purl.org/cs2013/LO/Support_for_concurrency_is_a_f> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                    cs2013:LearningOutcome ;
                                                           cs2013:hasKnowledgeUnit <http://purl.org/cs2013/KU/Concurrency_and_Parallelism> ;
                                                           cs2013:hasMasteryLevel <http://purl.org/cs2013/MasteryLevel/Familiarity> ;
                                                           rdfs:label "Support for concurrency is a fundamental programming-languages issue with rich material in programming language design, language implementation, and language theory. Due to coverage in other Knowledge Areas, this elective Knowledge Unit aims only to complement the material included elsewhere in the Body of Knowledge. Courses on programming languages are an excellent place to include a general treatment of concurrency including this other material." .


###  http://purl.org/cs2013/LO/Sustainability_is_characterize
<http://purl.org/cs2013/LO/Sustainability_is_characterize> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                    cs2013:LearningOutcome ;
                                                           cs2013:hasKnowledgeUnit <http://purl.org/cs2013/KU/Sustainability> ;
                                                           rdfs:label "Sustainability is characterized by the United Nations  as 'development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\" Sustainability was first introduced in the CS2008 curricular guidelines. Topics in this emerging area can be naturally integrated into other familiarity areas and units, such as human-computer interaction and software evolution. See cross-referencing with the Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) and Software Engineering (SE) Knowledge Areas." .


